# Cursor Workshop Repository Rules

## Project Type
This is an educational workshop repository teaching Cursor IDE features through hands-on exercises.

## Repository Structure
- main branch: Overview and getting started guide
- Feature branches (01-09): Each teaches a specific Cursor feature
- Each branch contains: BRANCH_NOTES.md, TASK.md, and sample code

## Branch Naming Convention
- Beginner: 01-03 (basic features)
- Intermediate: 04-06 (advanced features)
- Advanced: 07-09 (power user features)

## Content Guidelines

### BRANCH_NOTES.md
- Comprehensive explanation of the feature
- Use cases and examples
- Best practices
- Keyboard shortcuts
- Tips and tricks
- Links to next steps

### TASK.md
- Hands-on exercises
- Clear objectives
- Expected outcomes
- Self-assessment checklist
- Progressive difficulty

### Sample Code
- Intentionally simple for learning
- Demonstrates feature concepts
- Includes common patterns
- Has room for improvement (for practice)

## Code Style for Examples
- Clear and readable over clever
- Well-commented
- Demonstrates both good and bad practices (in appropriate files)
- Multiple difficulty levels

## Documentation Standards
- Write for beginners but include advanced tips
- Use clear, jargon-free language
- Provide examples for every concept
- Include visual cues (✅ ❌ 💡)
- Link related concepts

## Educational Approach
- Learn by doing
- Progressive complexity
- Immediate feedback
- Real-world applications
- Encourage experimentation

## Maintenance
- Keep content up-to-date with Cursor releases
- Update based on user feedback
- Ensure all exercises remain relevant
- Test exercises work as intended

